++
++# shape-plan
++
++A person names a business intent that `/shape-slice` has served with a slice. /shape-plan
++makes the intent's **one plan**, or brings it up to date: the plan file
++(`.garura/project/plans/<intent-issue>.yaml`), one **epic** for each slice the plan holds, and
++one **story** for each feature of that slice. Each story is written from its feature's ICE and
++that feature's capability's ICE, and holds no failure condition and no recovery condition:
++those stay in the slice's hidden file, which `/shape-slice` wrote. A second run makes no
++copies. The eval play (#798) comes next, once it is built.
++
++The run reads the slices from the spine (the link "slice serves business intent"); it never
++picks, orders or tests a slice. It finds each epic and story that already stands for a slice or
++a feature before it makes one, by the plan file's `represents` marks, never by a title. It
++shows the product owner one brief with every tracker write and the plan-file change, and
++writes nothing before the typed yes.
++
++**Pipeline position: both.** start-change opens the change at the head (on the plan's
++sub-branch when the intent has a plan); the end sequence commit-change → propose-change →
++check-change → merge-change lands it.
++
++## Compiled From
++
++This play was compiled from the shape-plan ICE (`reference/ice.md`) by create-play. Intent
++defines constraints (C1–C13) and failure conditions (F1–F11); the expectation defines success
++scenarios (S1–S7), a Done means (D1–D5, baked to `stop-condition.yaml`), and one recovery entry
++per failure condition. To modify this play, update `reference/ice.md` and recompile with
++edit-play.
++Do NOT edit this file manually — it is a compiled artifact.
++
++## Role
++
++You are the orchestrator. You own the step order, the one brief and the checkpoint. The play
++reads no memory itself. The `product-os-keeper` agent gathers context and calls the skill
++`author-work-items`, which writes the words of each epic and story; the agent passes the
++skill's output on as it is and never writes content. `template-manager` hands out the approval
++prompt and the close templates. You run all mechanical work through bundled scripts: the
++gather and the clean ICE copies (`gather_plan.py`, which runs the ready check
++`check_ice_workable.py`), the find (`find_work_items.py`), the draft check (`check_drafts.py`),
++the brief (`render_brief.py`), the checkpoint record and the write gate (`record_approval.py`),
++the tracker writes (`write_work_items.py`, through `platform_adapter.py`), the plan file
++(`write_plan_file.py`), the checks (`check_plan.py`, `check_tracker.py`), the write guard
++(`scoped_write_guard.py`) and the stop condition. Every script writes its files through a
++temp file and a rename (`plan_common.py`). The play never writes the plan file, an epic or a
++story by hand.
++
++**Forbidden:** a second plan for one intent (C1); picking, ordering or testing a slice, or
++leaving out a slice the spine links to the intent (C2); matching a work item by its title, or
++making an epic or a story that the plan file already marks (C3); deleting a story whose
++feature left the slice, or leaving it in the plan (C4); reading a "Fails if" line, or a story,
++an epic or a plan file that holds a failure or recovery condition (C5); an epic title that
++says how the product is built, or a map that disagrees with the tree (C6); writing outside the
++plan file, the epics and the stories — the evals folder and the product model above all (C7);
++a tracker write before the typed yes, a write the brief did not show, or a checkpoint skipped
++by config (C8); a story built from an ICE that is not ready, or a skipped feature not named
++(C9); landing outside the change plays or on the wrong branch (C10); a real person's name, or
++text a person cannot follow (C11); rewriting or deleting a line a person wrote on a found story
++(C12); a label on an epic or a story, or a wrong issue type (C13).
++
++**Agent boundaries:**
++
++| Agent | Domain | Skills it uses | Phases |
++|-------|--------|----------------|--------|
++| `product-os-keeper` | Gather context and pass it on: hand the skill `gather.json` and the clean ICE copies it names (never an ICE page), check that no clean copy holds a failure line, call the skill, and pass its `drafts.json` on as it is | `author-work-items` | Draft |
++| `template-manager` (utility) | Hand out the approval prompt and the close templates (evidence file, delivery report, Next-line map) into short-term memory | — | Checkpoint, Close |
++
++`product-os-keeper` is the single **domain agent**; `template-manager` is the one utility
++agent. The skill `author-work-items` was built for this play. The start and end sequence are
++injected sub-plays; everything else is a script.
++
++## Pre-flight
++
++| Check | Constraint | Action on Failure |
++|-------|-----------|-------------------|
++| Resolve config, `stm_base`, `product_base` (`.garura/core/config.yaml`) | — | Hard halt |
++| A business intent issue number was given (`/shape-plan <intent-issue>`, digits only); the plan's ID is that number | C1 | Hard halt — ask for the intent's issue number |
++| The product model's spine exists (`<product_base>product-os/_spine.yaml`) | C2 | Hard halt — run `/shape-slice <intent-issue>` first |
++| **Clean model tree** — after Step 0: `git status --porcelain -- <product_base>product-os` is empty | C7 | Hard halt (REC7) |
++| **Clean plans tree** — after Step 0: `git status --porcelain -- .garura/project/plans` is empty, so the write guard sees only this run's change and a restore never takes back a person's own plan edits | C7, C8 | Hard halt (REC7) |
++
++Resolve the facts with the bundled resolver, not by inference:
++
++```
++git branch --show-current
++python3 scripts/preflight.py --play shape-plan --config .garura/core/config.yaml \
++    --branch "<current branch>"
++```
++
++`preflight.py` gives `stm_base`, `product_base` and `evidence_record`. The change's own issue
++comes from start-change (Step 0), not from the branch name: the intent issue and the change
++issue are two issues. After Step 0, with `<issue>` = the change issue:
++
++```
++python3 scripts/session_stamp.py --phase start \
++    --marker "{stm_base}<issue>/status/session-stamp-shape-plan.json" \
++    --cwd "$(pwd)" --branch "$(git branch --show-current)"
++git rev-parse HEAD > {stm_base}<issue>/context/shape-plan/start-sha.txt
++shasum -a 256 .garura/project/plans/<intent-issue>.yaml > {stm_base}<issue>/context/shape-plan/plan-before.txt \
++    || echo "no plan file" > {stm_base}<issue>/context/shape-plan/plan-before.txt
++```
++
++Names used below: `<intent>` = `bi-<intent-issue>`, `<id>` = `<intent-issue>` (the plan's ID),
++`<model>` = `<product_base>product-os`, `<plans>` = `.garura/project/plans`,
++`<plan-file>` = `<plans>/<id>.yaml`, `<working>` = `{stm_base}<issue>/context/shape-plan/`.
++The stop condition reads `{stm_base}<issue>/`.
++
++**Resume check:** if `{stm_base}<issue>/status/shape-plan.json` exists, resume — skip
++completed steps, reset an in-progress step to pending, continue from the first incomplete one.
++
++## Task DAG
++
++Create ALL tasks right after pre-flight, before any domain work. The play owns this DAG.
++
++```
++[T0]  start-change (injected — start)                          blockedBy: []
++[T1]  Gather the slices and the clean ICE copies                blockedBy: [T0]
++[T2]  Find the epics and stories that exist                     blockedBy: [T1]
++[T3]  Draft the epics and stories                               blockedBy: [T2]
++[T4]  Check the drafts                                          blockedBy: [T3]
++[T5]  The brief and the checkpoint (yes / change / stop)        blockedBy: [T4]
++[T6]  Write the epics and stories in the tracker                blockedBy: [T5]
++[T7]  Write the plan file                                       blockedBy: [T6]
++[T8]  Check the plan, the tracker and the writes                blockedBy: [T7]
++[T9]  Commit the plan file                                      blockedBy: [T8]
++[E1]  commit-change (injected — end)                            blockedBy: [T9]
++[E2]  propose-change (injected — end)                           blockedBy: [E1]
++[E3]  check-change (injected — end)                             blockedBy: [E2]
++[E4]  merge-change (injected — end)                             blockedBy: [E3]
++[T10] Scenario Validation                                       blockedBy: [E4]
++[T11] Close                                                     blockedBy: [T10]
++```
++
++Mark each task in progress before its step and completed right after its eval passes. No
++runtime reordering. A **change** at the checkpoint sends the run back to T3; T3–T5 run again
++in order.
++
++## Workflow
++
++### Phase: Start (injected — D2 position: both, head)
++
++**Step 0 — start-change** · Owner: `start-change` (sub-play) · Depends on: pre-flight
++Run the start-of-pipeline member as a sub-play with `parent_run_id`. It resolves or creates
++the change issue, cuts its branch (the plan's sub-branch when the intent has a plan file,
++`<plans>/<id>.yaml`), and inits short-term memory. Then assert the clean model tree and the
++clean plans tree (hard halt if dirty — REC7).
++
++    { "play": "start-change", "parent_run_id": "<this run id>",
++      "inputs":  { "title": "[TASK] shape-plan: make the plan for business intent #<intent-issue>" },
++      "outputs": { "result": "<working>start-change.json" } }
++
++### Phase: Gather
++
++**Step 1 — Gather the slices and the clean ICE copies** · Owner: play (script) · Depends on: Step 0
++
++```
++python3 scripts/gather_plan.py --root <model> --spine <model>/_spine.yaml --intent <intent> \
++    --plans <plans> --out <working>gather.json
++```
++
++It lists every slice whose `intents` lists the intent, in the spine's order (C2), each
++slice's features in build order, each feature's capability and the pages of its two ICE. It
++runs the ready check (`check_ice_workable.py`) on both ICE of each feature: a feature whose
++ICE is not ready is `ready: false` with its `reason`, and gets no clean copy (C9). For each
++ready feature it writes `<working>ice/<feature id>.md`: both ICE with every "Fails if" line
++taken out (C5). The play and the skill read only these copies, never an ICE page. Exit 2
++means no slice serves the intent: tell the person to run `/shape-slice <intent-issue>` first,
++and close HALTED.
++**SE-1 (F2/C1/C2):** `gather.json` `plan_id` equals `<intent-issue>` and `plan_file` is
++`<plans>/<id>.yaml`, the one plan file for the intent; its `slices` are every spine slice whose
++`intents` lists `<intent>`, in spine order, none left out and none added.
++**SE-2 (F8/C9):** each feature with `ready: false` has a `reason` that is not empty and a null
++`clean_ice`; each feature with `ready: true` has a `clean_ice` file on disk; no other file is
++in `<working>ice/`.
++**SE-3 (F3/C5):** `grep -ril "fails if" <working>ice/` finds nothing, and no "Fails if" line of
++a feature's two ICE is in its clean copy.
++
++### Phase: Find
++
++**Step 2 — Find the epics and stories that exist** · Owner: play (script) · Depends on: Step 1
++
++```
++python3 scripts/find_work_items.py --gather <working>gather.json --plan-file <plan-file> \
++    --out <working>work-items.json
++```
++
++It reads the plan file and, through the code-host adapter, the tracker; it writes nothing.
++An epic is `use` when a node of the tree has `represents: <slice id>`, else `make`; a story is
++`use`, `add-sections` (it lacks "Scope of work" or "Context guidance"), `make`, or `skip` (its
++feature is not ready). It never compares titles (C3). `move_out` lists each marked story whose
++feature is no longer in the slice (C4). Exit 1 lists a duplicate (two nodes stand for one
++slice, or for one feature in one epic) or a marked issue the tracker does not have: show it to
++the person in plain words and stop (REC2); the run goes on only after the person fixes the
++plan file.
++**SE-4 (F2/C3):** `work-items.json` reads `ok: true`; its `duplicates` is empty; each `use` or
++`add-sections` item's issue is the `issue` of the plan-file node whose `represents` names that
++slice or feature; no item with a marked node is `make`.
++**SE-5 (F6/C4):** each node under an epic whose `represents` names a feature that is not in the
++slice is in that slice's `move_out`, and no feature of the slice is.
++
++### Phase: Draft
++
++**Step 3 — Draft the epics and stories** · Owner: `product-os-keeper` · Depends on: Step 2
++
++    { "task":    "have the epic of each slice and one story for each ready feature drafted. Hand author-work-items gather.json and the clean ICE copies it names (clean_ice); never open or hand on a page in ice_pages, and never read an ICE page yourself. First check that no clean copy holds the words 'Fails if' (grep -il 'fails if' on each); if one does, stop with step_failure and name it. Call author-work-items with gather, the clean copies, and, on a re-run, the problems of drafts-check.json (fix mode) or the product owner's change (change mode) with the earlier drafts.json. Pass its drafts.json on as it is. Gather and pass on only; the skill writes every word",
++      "inputs":  { "gather": "<working>gather.json", "clean_ice": "<working>ice/",
++                   "problems": "<working>drafts-check.json, on a fix", "change": "<the product owner's change, on a re-run>",
++                   "drafts": "<working>drafts.json, on a re-run" },
++      "outputs": { "drafts": "<working>drafts.json" },
++      "skills":  ["author-work-items"],
++      "task_id": "shape-plan-draft" }
++
++**Step 4 — Check the drafts** · Owner: play (script) · Depends on: Step 3
++
++```
++python3 scripts/check_drafts.py --drafts <working>drafts.json --gather <working>gather.json \
++    --out <working>drafts-check.json
++```
++
++It reads the original ICE pages itself, so the "Fails if" lines reach no skill and no person.
++Exit 1 lists each problem by slice and feature: send `drafts-check.json` back to the
++`shape-plan-draft` contract (fix mode), then run Step 4 again (REC3). The run does not go on
++until it reads ok.
++**SE-6 (F3/C5/C6):** `drafts-check.json` reads `ok: true`: each slice has an epic title; no
++text of an epic or a story holds a "Fails if" line of its two ICE, the words "Fails if", or a
++recovery phrase; each story has goals, limits, expectations and the four Context guidance
++lines.
++**SE-7 (F1/C9):** in `drafts.json`, each ready feature of each slice has exactly one story, and
++no story is for a feature that is not ready or not in its slice.
++
++### Phase: Checkpoint (class: one-way-door, pinned)
++
++**Step 5 — The brief and the checkpoint** · Owner: play (+ `template-manager` for the approval prompt) · Depends on: Step 4 · Checkpoint (class: one-way-door, **pinned** — the product owner decides; never skipped by config, `standards/rules/gate-config.md`)
++
++```
++python3 scripts/render_brief.py --gather <working>gather.json \
++    --work-items <working>work-items.json --drafts <working>drafts.json \
++    --out <working>brief.md
++```
++
++The script builds the whole brief from the run's records, so no write can be left out (C8):
++each epic and story the run will make, use, add sections to or move out, with the text a new
++item or section gets and each type, label or sub-issue change; the plan-file change; and each
++skipped feature with its reason (C9). The brief is never edited by hand: a problem in it goes
++back to the step whose record it reads. Frame the brief with the approval prompt from
++`template-manager` (`standards/templates/approval-prompt.md`) and show it. Ask the product
++owner to type **yes**, **change: <what>**, or **stop**. Save the reply word for word, and
++record it:
++
++```
++python3 scripts/record_approval.py --decision <yes|change|stop> --reply "<their exact words>" \
++    --at <now, UTC> --out <working>approval.json
++```
++
++- **yes** → Step 6.
++- **change** → nothing was written; re-run Step 3 with the change (change mode), then Steps 4
++  and 5; show the brief again.
++- **stop** → nothing was written, and nothing is written now. Write
++  `git status --porcelain -- <plans>` to `<working>stop-check.txt`, and
++  `shasum -a 256 <plan-file>` (or "no plan file") to `<working>plan-after-stop.txt`; close
++  HALTED. A stop needs no undo; if any write is found after a stop, undo it with
